# Data Classification Using AI

A supervised machine learning project developed as part of the **DecodeLabs Artificial Intelligence Industrial Training Program**, demonstrating an end-to-end classification workflow using an e-commerce orders dataset.

## Project Overview

The objective was to explore an e-commerce dataset, build a classification pipeline, compare multiple machine learning algorithms, and evaluate their ability to predict order statuses.

**Dataset:** 1,200 records | 14 columns  
**Problem Type:** Multiclass Classification  
**Target Variable:** `OrderStatus` (5 classes)

## ML Workflow

| Stage | Key Activities |
|---|---|
| **EDA** | Numerical and categorical analysis, target distribution, correlation analysis, and visualization. |
| **Data Cleaning** | Identified and handled missing values in `CouponCode`; checked invalid numerical values. |
| **Feature Selection** | Selected relevant numerical and categorical features while excluding unnecessary identifiers. |
| **Preprocessing** | Target encoding, numerical scaling, categorical encoding, and `ColumnTransformer`. |
| **Data Splitting** | 80% training (960 samples) and 20% testing (240 samples). |
| **Model Training** | Implemented and compared four classification algorithms. |
| **Evaluation** | Accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and confusion matrix analysis. |

## Models & Performance

| Model | Test Accuracy | Weighted F1 |
|---|---:|---:|
| Logistic Regression | 22.50% | 0.2227 |
| KNN | 21.25% | 0.1976 |
| Decision Tree | 21.67% | 0.2156 |
| Random Forest | 21.25% | 0.2091 |

## Key Findings & Challenges

- Observed limited predictive performance across all four models.
- Identified significant overfitting in the Decision Tree (100% training accuracy vs. 21.67% testing accuracy).
- Found that balanced target-class distribution does not necessarily indicate strong predictive relationships.
- Recognized potential limitations in the dataset's predictive information.

**Important:** Rather than artificially optimizing accuracy, I focused on understanding the data, evaluating model behavior, and honestly reporting the limitations of the classification task.

## Technologies Used

- Python
- Pandas & NumPy
- Matplotlib & Seaborn
- Scikit-learn
- Jupyter Notebook

## Key Learnings

- Exploratory Data Analysis and data quality assessment.
- Feature engineering and preprocessing pipelines.
- Model selection and comparative evaluation.
- Understanding overfitting and generalization.
- Interpreting model performance and dataset limitations.

